{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.BarEvenForcesStationarity","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.BarEvenForcesStationarity","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.Deriv.Inv","lean:module:Mathlib.Analysis.Calculus.Deriv.Comp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.BarEven.zD_deriv_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.BarEven.zD_deriv_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":115,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/BarEvenForcesStationarity.lean#L115-L121","doc":"**The contrast, stated together.**  `z_C` is bar-even hence stationary at the\nbar fixed point; `z_D` is not bar-even and is not stationary.  Since `α`'s `t²`\ncoefficient is `1/4 + z'(1)` and the `c·ln q` prediction is `1/4`, this is\nexactly why C passes the holonomy `t²` test and D fails it — and therefore why\nthat failure is not independent evidence against convention D. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.BarEven.deriv_zD_at_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.BarEven.deriv_zD_at_one","kind":"theorem","line":100,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/BarEvenForcesStationarity.lean#L100-L113","doc":"`z_D'(1) = 1 ≠ 0`, so nothing forces the `t²` match for convention D.\nStated as the explicit difference quotient limit's value via `deriv`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.BarEven.zD_not_barEven","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.BarEven.zD_not_barEven","kind":"theorem","line":95,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/BarEvenForcesStationarity.lean#L95-L98","doc":"**`z_D` is NOT bar-even**: at `q = 2` it takes the value `8/5` while\n`z_D(1/2) = (1/8)/(5/4) = 1/10`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.BarEven.zC_barEven","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.BarEven.zC_barEven","kind":"theorem","line":87,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/BarEvenForcesStationarity.lean#L87-L93","doc":"**`z_C` is bar-even**, as an identity for every nonzero `u`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.BarEven.zD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.BarEven.zD","kind":"def","line":84,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/BarEvenForcesStationarity.lean#L84-L85","doc":"The Markov parameter of **convention D**: `z_D = q³/(q²+1)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.BarEven.zC","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.BarEven.zC","kind":"def","line":79,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/BarEvenForcesStationarity.lean#L79-L82","doc":"The Markov parameter of **convention C**, in the square-root variable\n`u = √q`: `z_C = u/(u²+1)`.  Written this way it is manifestly invariant under\n`u ↦ u⁻¹`, which is the bar involution. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.BarEven.deriv_at_one_eq_zero_of_barEven","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.BarEven.deriv_at_one_eq_zero_of_barEven","kind":"theorem","line":50,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/BarEvenForcesStationarity.lean#L50-L77","doc":"**Bar-evenness forces stationarity at the bar fixed point.**\n\nIf `f` agrees with `x ↦ f x⁻¹` near `1` and is differentiable at `1`, then\n`deriv f 1 = 0`.\n\nThe proof is the one-line differentiation of `f (q⁻¹) = f q` at `q = 1`: the\nchain rule contributes the factor `d/dq (q⁻¹) = −1` there, giving\n`−f'(1) = f'(1)`.  Nothing about `f` beyond differentiability is used — the\nstatement is about the involution, not the function. ","signatureChunk":0}]}